parts, and an absolute part discards everything joined before it: joining
`"data"` and `"/records.rb"` is `"/records.rb"`, not `"data//records.rb"`. On
Windows a part that carries a drive but no root, such as `"D:other"`, also
replaces everything before it, as the host's `PathBuf::push` does. A path
value is accepted wherever these functions take a path, and `files.read`,
`files.write`, `files.append`, `files.exists`, `files.lines`, `files.delete`,
`files.copy`, `files.rename` and `bytes.write` accept either a path value or the
text form every existing program writes.

`path.parent` answers `nothing` for a root such as `/`, and for a bare filename
that has no directory part. A trailing separator names a directory without
changing its last name, so `path.filename("data/records/")` is `"records"` and
`path.stem` and `path.extension` read that same name. `path.resolve` makes a path
absolute against the working directory and removes `.` and `..` segments
lexically; it does not consult the filesystem, so it answers for a path that does
not exist yet. The WebAssembly playground has no working directory to resolve
against, so there `path.resolve` is refused with an `IoError` that names the
platform; the native `rb` binary performs the resolution described here.
`path.list` answers the names directly inside a directory, sorted, so the same
directory always prints the same list; an empty directory is an empty list rather
than an error, and a directory that does not exist is an `IoError`.

`files.copy` and `files.rename` refuse rather than write half a file: a copy
whose source is not there, or a rename onto a path that cannot be written, is an
`IoError` naming the path. `files.rename` moves as well as renames, so a copy
written to a temporary path and then renamed is one file at the end rather than
files.rename(from, to)   // Rename it, or move it

path.join("data", "records.rb")   // join with the platform's separator
path.parent("data/records.rb")    // "data", or nothing for a root
path.filename("data/records.rb")  // "records.rb"; a trailing separator is ignored
path.stem("data/records.rb")      // "records"
path.extension("data/records.rb") // "rb"
path.is_absolute("data")          // no
path.resolve("data")              // an absolute path with . and .. removed
path.list("data")                 // sorted entry names; [] for an empty directory
```

The files functions and `bytes.write` accept either a text path or a path value, so
`files.read(path.join("data", "records.rb"))` and
`files.read("data/records.rb")` are the same call. `path.join` uses the
platform's separator and lets an absolute part replace what came before it. The
WebAssembly playground has no working directory, so `path.resolve` is refused
there with an `IoError` naming the platform; run the native `rb` binary to
resolve a path.

